## The complete experience

An authorized member creates a counting application and issues a separate,
named invitation to each agent. An agent can redeem its link in a browser
or with the CLI. Each client retains its own key and exact enrollment
request before sending. After verified enrollment, it discovers the actual
application, prepares its local voice and explicitly becomes active.

Agents observe an open number and compete to promise that number. Artroom
admits one recorded promise. Only its verified holder speaks, locally, and
then records fulfillment. The stage shows promised and fulfilled numbers
separately. Refused competing claims are ordinary contention. Agents can
become inactive without leaving the room. Three initial agents and a fourth
joining are recording choreography, not a fixed population.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  Link[Named HTTPS invitation] --> Custody[Private key and exact Join custody]
  Custody --> Member[Verified native membership and session]
  Member --> App[Discover exact counting scope and pin]
  App --> Active[Arm local voice and activate]
  Active --> Claim[Propose commitment]
  Claim -->|Recorded acceptance verified| Speak[Speak on this agent's device]
  Claim -->|Known refusal| Wait[Wait or reread with bounded backoff]
  Claim -->|Unknown| Reconcile[Reconcile original signed request]
  Reconcile -->|Verified current promise| Speak
  Speak --> Fulfill[Retain completion and report fulfillment]
  Fulfill -->|Verified recorded fulfillment| Next[Open next number]
  Speak -->|Failure or uncertain interruption| Paused[Record failure or expire to pause]
```

## Selected application contract

Create a new declaration, canonical bytes and digest. Call its application
name `counting-commitments`. Preserve the existing `counting` declaration
and digest `sha256:435d9f5745048183d3cf0392ce909cd76909fd69ee368dfc57e142ba96af1d09`.
Existing scopes never acquire the new semantics. Establish a new app; the
stage may link to old history, but it must not silently migrate counters,
members, pending requests or audio attempts.

Use the existing declaration forms and restricted@1 evaluator. No Counting
rules go into Core, the relay or a central speaker coordinator. Establish
opens untimed configuration; Initialize separately opens the paused board.
The factory must not grant counting actions implicitly.

Items are configuration, board, participant and promise. Configuration and
board each have max 1. Participants have max 16 live rows, states inactive,
active and removed; removed is final. A MemberRef has at most one live
participant. Promise has max 1 live row, states pledged, fulfilled, failed,
cancelled and expired; only pledged is live. Final history remains subject
to the existing native storage/admission limits, not unlimited retention.

The exact act names and grants are:

| Act | Grant and selected behavior |
|---|---|
| `establish` | `counting.establish`; native creator supplies verified opener and target |
| `initialize` | `counting.control`; controller opens paused board |
| `participate` | `counting.participate`; signer opens their inactive participant |
| `activate` | `counting.activity`; participant's signer changes inactive to active |
| `deactivate` | `counting.activity`; signer changes active to inactive, with no live promise of theirs |
| `force-deactivate` | `counting.control`; controller changes another participant to inactive, with no live promise of theirs |
| `remove-participant` | `counting.control`; controller finalizes an inactive participant, with no live promise of theirs |
| `start` | `counting.control`; paused, no outstanding promise, opens claims for lastNumber + 1 |
| `commit` | `counting.commit`; eligible active signer opens the one pledge and binds it to the board |
| `fulfill` | `counting.fulfill`; that promise's signer records completion and advances lastNumber |
| `fail` | `counting.fail`; that promise's signer records local failure or uncertain speech and pauses |
| `cancel` | `counting.control`; controller cancels exact current pledge and pauses |
| `pause` | `counting.control`; pauses an open board that has no pledge |
| `reset` | `counting.control`; paused with no pledge, increments generation and clears count |

The controller's Pause button submits `cancel` when a pledge exists and
`pause` otherwise. Deactivating a current holder requires resolving their
promise first, then deactivating; these are two distinct recorded outcomes.
If the first outcome is unknown, reconcile it before issuing the second.
Membership removal is a different authority operation and does not erase
a pledge or imply the person was silent.

Board states are paused, open and finished. A pledged promise can coexist
only with an open board; the board references its exact item. Store target
1..100, last fulfilled number 0..100, generation 0..1,000,000 and serial
0..1,000,000. Each accepted commit proposes serial + 1, verified by the
scope. Promise records N, generation, serial, participant, signer MemberRef,
complete ordered active eligibility basis and deadline 30 seconds after
admission. The admission fact is the recorded commitment. Fulfillment is
a separate signed entry on the exact promise, with native expected revisions.
Use actual item/fact reference forms; do not embed unverified JSON as proof.

`commit` checks the complete ordered active basis using existing sameSet,
MemberRef correspondence and arithmetic rules. It checks signer ownership,
current generation/serial, N = lastNumber + 1 <= target, expected board and
participant revisions, no outstanding pledge and the fairness condition.
The sequencing boundary admits at most one competing proposal. Clients
may calculate fields, but the scope checks every proposed successor.

Fairness: if more than one participant is active, the last fulfilled speaker
cannot commit the immediately following number while they remain in that
active basis. With one active participant, that participant can continue.
This prevents consecutive successful monopolization; it does not guarantee
round robin or bounded service for every participant. Lower-latency agents
can still dominate among eligible peers. No hidden scheduler selects a winner.

`fulfill` checks exact pledge, signer, generation, serial, N and current board
link, native expected revisions, and admission before its recorded deadline.
It finalizes the promise, updates lastNumber and lastSpeaker, clears the board
link and opens the next number, or finishes at target. It does not select the
next speaker or require a new roster proposal. A duplicate request reconciles
to its original result; a new or late callback cannot fulfill a replacement.

`fail` and `cancel` resolve the exact pledged item, leave lastNumber unchanged
and pause the board through their explicit also.board subject. Native timed
rules have no other subject (derive validate/timed.ts 72..88), so expiry uses
two own-item rows with the same recorded deadline: `commitment-expired` on
the open board pauses it and clears its pledge link/deadline; `promise-expired`
on the pledged promise finalizes that promise as expired. Commit copies the
same admission-time-plus-30-seconds value to both items. Fulfill/fail/cancel
clear the board deadline. Start and commit require both no live pledge and
no board pledge link. Either timer may be recorded first; the intermediate
state remains closed to new commitments. A controller waits for both actual
outcomes before Start. A bounded native witness must cover both orders,
reactivation and late callbacks; no atomic two-item timed entry is claimed.
Neither timer opens a replacement. Record failure reason from the closed set
media-error, process-error, cancelled, interrupted, speech-uncertain. Timed
expiry means no fulfillment was recorded in time, not that no sound occurred.
The controller assesses interruption/ongoing sound before Start permits a
new promise for the same N. Reset is allowed only after outstanding work is
resolved; it changes generation and retains old obligations and requests.

With no active participants, an open board waits; agents perform no polling
spin or claims. Activation wakes observers. With no eligible member because
of an authority/refusal problem, show waiting/attention and preserve state.
Revocation and membership removals apply through actual native authority and
session windows; do not promise instant propagation across scopes. A local
known access failure immediately stops that runner's new work and audio.

One current recorded pledge restricts compliant agents' concurrent speech.
It is not a guarantee against audio continuing on a disconnected/crashed
device, nor acoustic exactly-once delivery. Pausing/replacing an uncertain
pledge must keep that distinction visible.

## Admission and explicit cohort

Select a maximum of 16 live native member rows including the founder,
pending named invitations, active members and inactive counting members.
Invitations consume a slot when issued; redemption changes that same row
and does not consume another slot. Lapsed or removed final rows release
live capacity, but their history remains retained. Repeat redemption must
not create another member. Counting activity never releases membership
capacity. Bound native live key rows to 32, including pending key invitations;
key revocation/expiry releases live capacity by existing final states.

Use native item max admission, including all opening paths, rather than a
client-only limit. Do not alter membership@1/@2/@4. Add explicit membership@5
with these declared bounds and unchanged native invitation/recovery rules.
Create a dedicated explicit counting cohort: register@6, directory@6,
membership@5, rules@3, destination@2, inbox@1. The new register/directory
declare that exact closure. Existing APPLICATION_COHORT and legacy defaults
stay as currently pinned. If these version labels are already allocated
when authoring begins, reserve and report a single corrected explicit tuple
before code; never use newest aliases or mutate an old pin.

The selected agent role gets counting.participate/activity/commit/fulfill/fail
through an explicit authorized set-actions setup. Controller retains control
and invitation authority. This setup is recorded and verified before links
are issued. Role does not authorize an operator to sign for an agent.
Native member/key kind taxonomy stays truthful to the existing invite/join
contract; separate keys and MemberRefs establish independent participants.

Initial admission is named one-time invitations issued by authorized members.
There is no public self-service issuer, reusable personal secret or automatic
anonymous registration. A public room entrance/admission queue follows later.
Invitation lifetime defaults to one hour for the demo; existing authorized
issuance can choose another bounded native-valid lifetime explicitly.

## One URL, shared parsing and enrollment custody

Select `https://<configured-service-origin>/join/#artroom-invite=<payload>`.
Payload is base64url canonical UTF-8 JSON, closed schema `v:2`, containing
service origin, trusted deployment identifier, complete directory/membership/
rules/destination ScopeRefs, exact supported membership pin, invitation ID,
secret, handle, and application {scope: full ScopeRef, definition: Digest}.
All identities are hints until verified through native results and retained
creation provenance. URL opening or discovery never redeems an invitation.

Bound the whole URL to 8 KiB UTF-8, decoded JSON to 6 KiB, handle to its actual
native bound and secret to the native 32..256 UTF-8 range. Issuers generate
32 random bytes encoded base64url. Reject unknown/duplicate fields, malformed
base64url/UTF-8, wrong types, unsafe IDs, userinfo, query secrets, unexpected
path, unsupported exact pins, non-HTTPS origins and configured-origin mismatch.
Keep supported old `artroom-invite:` v1 inputs through an explicit bounded
compatibility adapter; missing old pins mean membership@1, never newest.

Browser routes to its configured API/deployment only. CLI routes to the exact
origin explicitly named by the join command, bound to configured trust or
the installation's verified host discovery. Do not follow arbitrary redirects
or fetch URLs supplied inside payload fields. Read tokens stay in Authorization.
Secrets stay in the fragment and private custody, never query strings, logs,
referrers, workroom messages or screenshots. The landing page has no third-party
requests. Retain provisional private invitation state before removing the
fragment with replaceState; failure retains a local recovery path and sends
no Join. Only explicit Join generates/retains the local signer and request.

Move the shared codec to `packages/client/src/invitation.ts` with typed exports
parseInvitation, formatInvitation and validateInvitationBinding. CLI Link/
linkOf becomes a compatibility facade, not a second validator. Page placeOf,
admittedInvitation and counting startup use the same codec. It verifies
encoding/configuration, not authority; native Join remains authoritative.

Reuse the existing browser Join journal owner and its global metadata lock,
generation fences and bounded private records. Do not implement a second
enrollment store. Preserve key-bound unsigned preparation, exact signed
envelope before POST, request digest, link binding, accepted receipt and
verified original admitted entry. Save/read back under lock before sending.
Lost reply/reload reconciles the original envelope and key. No replacement
identity/signature on storage failure or unknown outcome. A key invitation
for the same member is separate from a new member invitation; cross-device
recovery remains under the original identity/device story.

After verified Join, obtain an actual supported native session, verify the
directory's application creation result/full reference/pin and actual standing,
then open the application. No private CLI key file or hand-copied binding JSON
is required. Participate creates an inactive row; voice readiness and explicit
activation are separate. Reopening a retained enrollment recovers that same
identity and participant, not another Join/Participate.

Proposed commands, to be implemented rather than advertised as current:

```sh
artroom invite @ada --role agent --application counting-commitments --hours 1
artroom join 'https://service.example/join/#artroom-invite=ENCODED_PAYLOAD'
artroom count run --voice 'Samantha'
artroom count deactivate
```

The named application option selects exactly one verified directory record of
that name and supported digest; ambiguous matches refuse and require an explicit
full scope argument. `count run` uses the retained verified binding from Join,
opens the local voice and requests activation with the user's explicit command.
Stage invitations supply a selected exact app; no guessing latest application.

## One logical runner and two local speech adapters

Implement a recorded-only Application facade from the reviewed live-interface
contract: discover/read/invoke/reconcile/watch/close. Reuse ScopeHandle,
DeclaredHandle, actual settlement, U1 prepared-request custody and W1 observation.
No new watch, signer or session owner. Socket/live-region implementation is
outside this counting slice. Unsupported transport capabilities stay explicit.

Expose the selected four MCP names artroom_discover, artroom_read,
artroom_invoke and artroom_wait through that facade. MCP invoke input is a
closed union: mode submit with act/prepared request, or mode reconcile with
the existing requestId. Reconcile calls the same private custody owner and
native settlement as Application.reconcile; it never submits a replacement.
This adapter refinement must be reconciled with Draft2 during review. Keep
existing protocol tools supported. A host without an authorized local signer
has read/wait and a truthful invoke refusal. MCP does not execute speech.

Logical runner input is the exact binding, its local signer/custody, Application
and a SpeechAdapter {prepare, speak, cancel}. One local runner per signer/app
uses an exclusive private lock. Each attempt has a random local audio ID bound
to the accepted promise/full admission fact, never merely a displayed N.

Observe/read → eligible commit → retain exact envelope → invoke → reconcile
or verify actual accepted receipt/entry/current pledge → retain audio-start
marker before side effect → speak → retain completion → fulfill exact pledge.
Before speech, obtain a fresh complete authorized view confirming this current
promise, deadline, generation and active participant. A head alone settles
neither commit nor fulfillment. Unknown commit stays silent until resolved.
Unknown fulfillment never causes speech again. After a persisted start marker
and interrupted runner, local speech is uncertain and is never automatically
replayed, even if the scope still shows the pledge.

Contention: at most one in-flight signed claim per runner. Known refusal permits
a fresh read and randomized 250..1000 ms backoff; at most 8 consecutive attempts
without a useful head change, then wait/attention. No busy loop. Unknown claim
permits only reconciliation of that exact request. Invalid/access failures stop
claims, with normal native window qualifications. The fairness rule is server
checked; random backoff is a load-control aid, not fairness proof.

Browser SpeechAdapter requires explicit user gesture, available selected voice
and readiness before activation. Bind end/error/cancel to audio ID and exact
promise; duplicate/late callbacks are ignored by custody, not by changing N.
Use the existing U1 journal bounds (32 slots, 64 KiB each, at most 8 retained
attempts), preserving history and prior unknown outcomes. Quota failure prevents
new speech. Cancellation cannot complete another promise or discard uncertainty.

macOS SpeechAdapter invokes `/usr/bin/say` with an argument vector, selected
voice and decimal N text, through this agent's authorized local execution.
Completion means actual zero process exit; nonzero/cancel maps to fail. Track
the owned child handle, cancel only it, and join its termination before local
restart. Do not claim a killed/lost process was silent. Local host permissions
remain explicit; running the CLI does not grant a remote MCP service shell access.
The local host can connect MCP room tools and execute say under its own authority.

Both report authenticated local completion, not a microphone measurement or
proof that every listener heard sound. Browser close/disconnection leaves native
work and pending custody intact; the pledge eventually expires to pause. Other
devices observe it through native state. Enrolled inactive members remain visible.

